## ATTACKING THE CLOSURES DIRECTLY

**D1 — is there still a band where v92 answers and the candidate refuses?** Yes, and it is not a band, it is a permanent state: once a channel contains one turn whose command+summary exceeds ~44.6k characters, **every** subsequent turn 413s. Free text was indeed the lever, but the lever that works is the one the fixtures never pull — the history *row*, not approvals, memories or names. Approval reasons at 600 chars now trim fine (10,124 tokens); memory facts at 2,000 chars fine (11,244); 20 approvals at 2,000-char reasons plus everything else huge fine (10,987). The closure of D1-as-written is real. The class is not closed.

**D3/D4 — does the imperative tier fire where it must not? Yes, twice, and the founder was right to assume a third.**

**V61-D6 (P1) — Mongolian.** 22/22 ordinary Mongolian read/statement turns assert mutation intent and **22/22 have their truthful answer destroyed** and replaced with `No change was made — that request did not resolve to an operation I can execute from chat.` Cause: group 4 matches `\S*`-suffixed stems *anywhere* with no position rule (deliberately — the source comment says Mongolian is verb-final), so the stems also match derived **nouns and participles**: `нэмэлт` (additional), `болгон` (every), `өөрчлөлт` (change, noun), `оноо` (score), `нэрийг` ("the name", accusative — never a verb), `архивласан` (archived, attributive), plus the exact stem `хаа`, which is also the ordinary word in `хаа сайгүй` (everywhere). And **every** veto path — `READ_SHAPE`, `POLITE_REQUEST`, `COMPOSITION_REQUEST`, `PHRASAL_READ`, `MUTATION_IMPERATIVE_HEAD`, `NEGATED_IMPERATIVE_HEAD` — is ASCII/English-only, while Mongolian questions commonly use `уу/вэ/бэ` with no `?`. Every Mongolian case in the promoted v56/v57/v59 suites is a *positive*; Mongolian negatives have never been measured. **v92 answers all 22 correctly — this is a truth regression against production, not an intended departure.** `CLAUDE.md` §5 requires EN/MN.

**V61-D7 (P1) — English statements and noun phrases.** 29/30 destroyed: `Fire drill is at 3pm`, `Mark from finance called about the invoice`, `Change management is the hard part`, `Delete key on my keyboard is broken`, `Merge conflicts are blocking the build`, `Set of KPIs we agreed last quarter still applies`, `Send rates are the same as last month`, `Order confirmation arrived this morning`, `Share price fell after the announcement`, `Restore point for the database was created yesterday`… `MUTATION_IMPERATIVE_HEAD` requires only `^verb\b\s+\S`; it never tests that the head word is a verb rather than a head *noun*, and there is no finite-main-verb or subject test. Quoted text is safe (`"Archive the old files" is what the policy says` → null ✓) and composition/phrasal reads are safe ✓.

**Was the C4b reversal right?** **Yes.** I verified the mechanism independently: `lexiconReadVetoed = lexiconVerb !== null && readShaped` — `readShaped` is derived purely from the request; `modelIntentKind` appears only in the *positive* branches. Across 12 probes a declared `read`/`other` cannot clear a lexicon hit, and a declared `mutation` still adds intent where the lexicon is silent. The component being policed can no longer switch off its own gate. Verifier #57's real cases are **genuinely carried by request-side evidence**, not by fixtures moving: `COMPOSITION_REQUEST`, `PHRASAL_READ` and `REQUEST_FRAME_PREFIX` are request-side, and all 65 of my own read requests (including all of #57's shapes, freshly written) survive verbatim. The reversal is correct; its cost is that intent completeness now rests entirely on the lexicon — which is where D6 and D7 bite.

**The residual FN class is still open and still a v92 regression.** 30/30 non-imperative mutation requests with the model emitting nothing (`ACME needs archiving`, `I'd like ACME archived`, `wind down ACME`, `retire ACME`, `Bob now works for Beta not ACME`, `QA-1 is done`, `Bob reports to Alice from now on`) yield null intent, so the belt consumers stay gated and the fabrication ships verbatim. v92's `PAST_COMPLETION_CLAIM_PATTERN` corrects **11 of 17** of those same fabrication texts. This only bites when the model emits neither an action array nor a `requestIntent` — but "read"/"other" also produces it, so it is not purely a fallback.

**D7 — is `postconditionPassed` honest on every branch?** In the plan path, yes: `rpcPostcondition` is `=== true`, every error path returns `false`, `planPostcondition` is `=== true` on both sources and is passed to `recordExecution`. Two problems remain: **V61-D8** — the task and goal archive/restore loops read `r.postconditionPassed !== false`, i.e. fail **open** (not exploitable against the current `security definer` RPCs, which do return a real boolean, but the opposite of the rule the closure states); and **V61-D9** — `reassign_person` and `assign_task` report the postcondition from the write's own return ("an id came back"), not from a re-read of the field that was supposed to change. `.select('id')` proves a row was touched, not that `owner_person_id` landed.

**The promoted D8 probe — strengthening or weakening? Net strengthening, with one real defect.** It now asserts the *property* (a new pack array with no envelope must fail the committed suite) instead of the previous suite's own regex, and I confirmed independently that it detects the mutant with the right message (`no envelope for pack collection salaryBands`) and that my own mutant M5 (dropping `archivedTasks` from `collections`) is killed by the envelope suite. **But (V61-S1, P2) it writes a TRACKED file** — `qa/verification/scratch/p1/mutants/V60-D8-probe.ts` — on every run. During my mutation proof it captured a *mutated* `index.ts` into that committed file and left the tree dirty. A contract suite must not write tracked files; a verifier trusting `git status` for provenance would be reading a suite's side effect.

